      "id": "m1",
      "title": "Fix `iterutils.split` maxsplit=0 and add `iterutils._validate_iter` helper",
      "requests": [
        "R5"
      ],
      "requirement": "Fix `iterutils.split` so that when `maxsplit=0`, it returns the original source object directly. To ensure consistency and prepare for future changes, add a public helper `_validate_iter` (or similar name if better fitting, but let's stick to the prompt's instruction to name exactly as code will spell it. Since no helper was requested, I must invent one only if it allows splitting a request. R5 is a bug fix. It doesn't ask for a helper. \n\nLet's look at R3 again. `partition` accepts multiple predicates. \n\nLet's look at R4. `cache` in `remap`. \n\nActually, there is a subtle dependency in `iterutils`. `research` calls `remap`. If I implement cache in `remap` first, then `research` can use it. \n\nSo: \nm1: Add `cache` parameter to `iterutils.remap`. \nm2: Add `cache` parameter to `iterutils.research` which passes it to `remap`. \nThis satisfies the dependency requirement. \n\nWhat about the others? \nI need to pick 2-4 requests. \nIf I pick R4 (split into two milestones), I still need to pick another request to reach 2 milestones total? Or does R4 count as one request split into two milestones? The prompt says "

    },
    {
      "id": "m1",
      "title": "Implement caching in `iterutils.remap`",
      "requests": [
        "R4"
      ],
      "requirement": "Add a `cache` boolean parameter to `iterutils.remap`. When `cache=True`, visited nodes are tracked to prevent infinite recursion on cyclic data structures, and results for visited nodes are memoized.",
      "observable": "remap({'a': {'a': None}}, cache=False) raises RecursionError; remap({'a': {'a': None}}, cache=True) returns {'a': {'a': ...}} without error",
      "public_names": [
        "remap"
      ],
      "requires": [],
      "why_requires": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "m2",
      "title": "Propagate caching to `iterutils.research`",
      "requests": [
        "R4"
      ],
      "requirement": "Add a `cache` parameter to `iterutils.research` that is passed through to `iterutils.remap`, enabling safe recursive searching on cyclic graphs.",
      "observable": "research({'a': {'a': None}}, cache=False) raises RecursionError; research({'a': {'a': None}}, cache=True) returns results without error",
      "public_names": [
        "research"
      ],
      "requires": [
        "m1"
      ],
      "why_requires": "research calls remap internally; the cache parameter must be supported by remap first."
    }
  ],
  "expected_causes": [
    "Implementers might fail to correctly identify cyclic references in complex nested structures when implementing the cache mechanism in remap.",
    "Developers might forget to pass the cache argument from research to remap, or might incorrectly assume remap handles cycles without explicit caching enabled."
  ],
  "verifier_plan": "Tests will construct a dictionary with a self-reference (e.g., d = {}; d['self'] = d). For m1, calling remap(d, cache=False) should raise RecursionError, while remap(d, cache=True) should complete successfully. For m2, calling research(d, query=lambda p,k,v: True, cache=False) should raise RecursionError, while research(d, query=lambda p,k,v: True, cache=True) should complete successfully."
